[17592186405113 :answer-field/choices] [17592186405120 :answer-field/id] [17592186405120 :answer-field/key] [17592186405120 :answer-field/type] [17592186405120 :answer-field/correct] [17592186405120 :answer-field/choices] [17592186405127 :answer-field/id] [17592186405127 :answer-field/key] [17592186405127 :answer-field/type] [17592186405127 :answer-field/correct] [17592186405127 :answer-field/choices] [17592186405134 :answer-field/id] [17592186405134 :answer-field/key] [17592186405134 :answer-field/type] [17592186405134 :answer-field/correct] [17592186405134 :answer-field/choices] [17592186405141 :answer-field/id] [17592186405141 :answer-field/key] [17592186405141 :answer-field/type] [17592186405141 :answer-field/correct] [17592186405141 :answer-field/choices] [17592186405148 :answer-field/id] [17592186405148 :answer-field/key] [17592186405148 :answer-field/type] [17592186405148 :answer-field/correct] [17592186405148 :answer-field/choices] [17592186405155 :answer-field/id] [17592186405155 :answer-field/key] [17592186405155 :answer-field/type] [17592186405155 :answer-field/correct] [17592186405155 :answer-field/choices] [17592186405162 :answer-field/id] [17592186405162 :answer-field/key] [17592186405162 :answer-field/type] [17592186405162 :answer-field/correct] [17592186405162 :answer-field/choices] [17592186405169 :answer-field/id] [17592186405169 :answer-field/key] [17592186405169 :answer-field/type] [17592186405169 :answer-field/correct] [17592186405169 :answer-field/choices] [17592186405176 :answer-field/id] [17592186405176 :answer-field/key] [17592186405176 :answer-field/type] [17592186405176 :answer-field/correct] [17592186405176 :answer-field/choices] [17592186405190 :answer-field/id] [17592186405190 :answer-field/key] [17592186405190 :answer-field/type] [17592186405190 :answer-field/correct] [17592186405190 :answer-field/choices] [17592186405204 :answer-field/id] [17592186405204 :answer-field/key] [17592186405204 :answer-field/type] [17592186405204 :answer-field/correct] [17592186405204 :answer-field/choices] [17592186405211 :answer-field/id] [17592186405211 :answer-field/key] [17592186405211 :answer-field/type] [17592186405211 :answer-field/correct] [17592186405211 :answer-field/choices]] "retractions" [[17592186405038 :question/problem "Given that the tangent line approximation to the function $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2},$ which of the following must be true?"] [17592186405038 :question/worked-solution "Since the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\sqrt{2}$ overestimates the values of the function, we have\n\n$$\nL(x)>f(x),\n$$\n\nthat is, the line $L(x)$ must be above the graph of $f(x)$ near the point  $x=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the function must be concave down in the neighborhood of $x=\\sqrt{2}.$"] [17592186405045 :question/problem "Which of the following sequences diverge?\n\nI. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{1-{n}^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{n}$ for $n\\ge 1$\nIII. ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2{n}^{2}-3{n}^{3}}{{n}^{3}+5}$ for $n\\ge 1$"] [17592186172295 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\pi /2\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2$ ?"] [17592186172295 :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({sec}^{3}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{-\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta +9{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta (1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186405058 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi $ ?"] [17592186405058 :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta })^{2}+{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )+\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡4\\theta }{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )^{2}+4(1-{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta )}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )^{2}+4(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )[1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta +4-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ]}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186405065 :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+2{x}^{2}y\\,dy={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?"] [17592186405072 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $F(x,y)=⟨xy-\\sqrt[3]{x+5},\\,x+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?"] [17592186405079 :question/problem "![](images/65/6571fcdf94818485c2a39e90e018d46e9c95e1acbe0a8cf2f67c688d160a347c.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*"] [17592186405079 :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186198625 :question/problem "![](images/e6/e61f3d7c23ef7876bec2f11c93974551058bcd32f28745898604926d84dbcfee.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨y,\\,0,\\,2x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?"] [17592186198626 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,z,\\,0⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinders $z=4{x}^{2}$ and $z=16-4{y}^{2}.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?"] [17592186405098 :question/problem "![](images/14/1422e6534c001214335dcddb8888f7e2e9344e440379c47da38514e9a3ef0bd8.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,2⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?"] [17592186405105 :question/problem "![](images/6a/6af3d84594e2b2648dcbbae05a64f03ef5a38c76f5a1ed835fc4c6b6f50ef97a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,-x,\\,-z⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-3\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?"] [17592186405112 :question/problem "![](images/c7/c7f140ad2c5687db48171819e1efaa858701af881ebbee57ff835da569f30947.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?"] [17592186405119 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,-z,\\,2y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the cone $z=4-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=0.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?"] [17592186405126 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨-y,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the hemisphere $z=1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=1.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?"] [17592186405126 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=1$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane ${S}_{1}.$\n\nSince the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨0,0,1⟩.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨-y,0,x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower hemisphere ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=-y,\\,Q=0,\\,R=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-(0)\\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}-x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186405133 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨0,\\,x,\\,y⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the hemisphere $z=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=1-\\frac{1}{2}x.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?"] [17592186405133 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface ${S}_{1}:z=g(x,y)=\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ is oriented upward, and\n\n- the lower surface ${S}_{2}:z=h(x,y)=1-\\frac{1}{2}x$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper hemisphere ${S}_{1}.$\n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=x,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})-x\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}})+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Consider lower plane ${S}_{2}.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{1}{2}x)=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂h}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{2}x)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $F=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩,$ then\n\n$$\nP=0,\\,Q=x,\\,R=y.\n$$\n\n \n\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & =-{∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-x\\cdot 0+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}+y)\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186405140 :question/problem "![](images/2f/2fff2eab1f5670dfcd3351dcbb9be1b3110e6ebdcd452cf4c5a273611d454c8b.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?"] [17592186405147 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the cone $z=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the parabolic cylinder $z={y}^{2}-2.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?"] [17592186405154 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j+t\\,k$ at the point $P(-1,0,\\frac{3}{2}),$ valid for real values of $u?$"] [17592186405154 :question/worked-solution "The point $P(-1,0,\\frac{3}{2})$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r({t}_{0})+u\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =-i+0\\,j+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j+t\\,k & =-i+0\\,j+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t=-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t=0 \\\\ t=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=\\frac{3}{2}.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(-1,0,\\frac{3}{2})$ is at the tip of the vector $r(\\frac{3}{2}).$  \n\nWe calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{3}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =0\\,i+\\pi \\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =\\pi \\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P(-1,0,\\frac{3}{2})$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=-i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,k+u\\,(\\pi \\,j+\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"] [17592186405161 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1$ at the point $(0,0,2).$"] [17592186405168 :question/problem "A tank is being filled with water. The rate of change of the volume of water in the tank $V,$ in milliliters, is directly proportional to the time $t,$ measured in hours. Additionally, the tank is also leaking $200\\,\\text{ml}$ of water every hour. What is the differential equation that describes the rate of change of the volume of water in the tank, at time $t,$ where $t$ is the time (in hours) since the tank started filling?"] [17592186405175 :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are **not** in row echelon form?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186405175 :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not in row echelon form since ${b}_{22}\\ne 0$ (condition II is violated).\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is **not** in row echelon form."] [17592186405182 :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x+6y+12=0$"] [17592186405182 :question/worked-solution "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts.  \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $x,$ if possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x+6y+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2x+6\\cdot 0+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+12 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+1+12 & =0+1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+12 & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x-1)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all values of $x,$ there is no $x$ such that $(x-1)^{2}=-11.$ Therefore, circle has no $x$-intercepts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ea/ea1410ea792ac5fb09316bc6d417c2ba46f84c050491b1e351f360eac50deb6c.png)"] [17592186405182 :question/answer-fields 17592186405183] [17592186405183 :answer-field/choices 17592186405184] [17592186405183 :answer-field/choices 17592186405185] [17592186405183 :answer-field/choices 17592186405186] [17592186405183 :answer-field/choices 17592186405187] [17592186405183 :answer-field/choices 17592186405188] [17592186405183 :answer-field/correct 17592186405184] [17592186405183 :answer-field/id #uuid "49673678-7bc5-5316-be28-063bdf75dac5"] [17592186405183 :answer-field/key "selection"] [17592186405183 :answer-field/type 17592186045493] [17592186405184 :answer/id #uuid "736006b2-c984-5f16-99e0-7bede131ce8f"] [17592186405184 :answer/type 17592186045500] [17592186405184 :answer/value "The circle has no $x$-intercepts"] [17592186405185 :answer/id #uuid "bab5e31a-96b2-50df-a788-c26d61f45ce5"] [17592186405185 :answer/type 17592186045499] [17592186405185 :answer/value "(2,0)"] [17592186405186 :answer/id #uuid "8e106efb-4b05-5489-9405-38ad43685dd3"] [17592186405186 :answer/type 17592186045500] [17592186405186 :answer/value "$(1-\\sqrt{11},0)$ and $(1+\\sqrt{11},0)$"] [17592186405187 :answer/id #uuid "77d6e2e0-104a-585f-8b8c-f66417a730a9"] [17592186405187 :answer/type 17592186045500] [17592186405187 :answer/value "$(2,0)$ and $(5,0)$"] [17592186405188 :answer/id #uuid "1aa8b8a3-06d9-587f-b744-e844d9ffa161"] [17592186405188 :answer/type 17592186045499] [17592186405188 :answer/value "(\\sqrt{11},0)"] [17592186405189 :question/problem "The rate of growth of a car's displacement increases at a rate that's proportional to the time $t$ since the car started moving. What differential equation describes the rate of change of the car's displacement, $D,$ at time $t?$"] [17592186405189 :question/worked-solution "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The rate of growth of a **car's displacement** $D(t)$ **increases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the **time** $t$ since the car started moving.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *car's displacement* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}$ is *proportional to* the *interval of time* since the car started moving. In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}∝t.\n$$\n\nRecall that $∝$ means \"is proportional to.\"\n\n- Finally, we want to replace the proportionality symbol with an equality symbol. Let $k$ be a positive constant. Since the car's displacement *increases* with time, our derivative should be *positive*. Therefore, we can rewrite our proportionality statement as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}=kt.\n$$"] [17592186405196 :question/answer-fields 17592186405197] [17592186405197 :answer-field/choices 17592186405198] [17592186405197 :answer-field/choices 17592186405199] [17592186405197 :answer-field/choices 17592186405200] [17592186405197 :answer-field/choices 17592186405201] [17592186405197 :answer-field/choices 17592186405202] [17592186405197 :answer-field/correct 17592186405202] [17592186405197 :answer-field/id #uuid "f5283d39-dcd8-5df6-aae7-c396d0691e3c"] [17592186405197 :answer-field/key "selection"] [17592186405197 :answer-field/type 17592186045493] [17592186405198 :answer/id #uuid "4635909d-d1cb-573a-bd44-d8c587978343"] [17592186405198 :answer/type 17592186045499] [17592186405198 :answer/value "y=1+(x-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{3}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯"] [17592186405199 :answer/id #uuid "1136a0bc-37ae-5098-9fa3-ad53832c8724"] [17592186405199 :answer/type 17592186045499] [17592186405199 :answer/value "y=1-(x-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{3}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯"] [17592186405200 :answer/id #uuid "f096707f-5894-5389-997f-94a4fa989704"] [17592186405200 :answer/type 17592186045499] [17592186405200 :answer/value "y=1-(x-\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{3}+\\frac{1}{3}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯"] [17592186405201 :answer/id #uuid "b86c8e20-b728-52b1-b463-0e009c18ac02"] [17592186405201 :answer/type 17592186045499] [17592186405201 :answer/value "y=1+(x-\\pi )+\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}-\\frac{1}{9}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯"] [17592186405202 :answer/id #uuid "2016a2e2-9e4e-5a38-9fa3-a7d1bc597b90"] [17592186405202 :answer/type 17592186045499] [17592186405202 :answer/value "y=1-(x-\\pi )-\\frac{1}{6}(x-\\pi )^{3}+⋯"] [17592186405203 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=7.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ find the principal axes of the curve."] [17592186405210 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=7.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ find the principal axes of the curve."] [17592186405210 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=7\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$"]]}
